WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.145
Aliasing means multiple pointers can refer to the same allocation.

2
00:00:04.145 --> 00:00:13.089
One pointer may be the owner, another may be borrowed by a helper, another may be stored in a structure, and another may point to an element or member.

3
00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:20.216
When cleanup happens, the allocation's lifetime ends for all of them, not just for the variable used in the free call.

4
00:00:20.216 --> 00:00:24.142
Setting one pointer to NULL after cleanup can be useful locally.

5
00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:32.068
It can prevent accidental reuse of that specific variable and make a simple cleanup section safer to call after partial progress.

6
00:00:32.068 --> 00:00:34.686
However, it does not update other aliases.

7
00:00:34.686 --> 00:00:39.849
Any copied pointer values still exist and must be governed by the lifetime contract.

8
00:00:39.849 --> 00:00:42.249
This distinction matters in real code.

9
00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:47.194
A structure may keep a child pointer while a temporary variable also refers to it.

10
00:00:47.194 --> 00:00:51.193
A callback may borrow a pointer while the owner is being torn down.

11
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:56.283
A container may store an object and the caller may still have the original variable.

12
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:59.992
Nulling one variable does not resolve those relationships.

13
00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:04.646
Pointer-to-pointer cleanup can sometimes clarify caller-visible state.

14
00:01:04.646 --> 00:01:12.500
A destroy helper that accepts an address of the owning pointer can release the object and set the caller's owning pointer to NULL.

15
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:19.989
That can be a good local convention, but it should be documented and should not be treated as a substitute for alias control.

16
00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:22.825
The reliable control is lifetime discipline.

17
00:01:22.825 --> 00:01:31.188
Know which pointer owns the allocation, which pointers merely borrow it, when the lifetime ends, and which aliases must stop being used.

18
00:01:31.188 --> 00:01:35.551
NULL assignment can support that discipline, but it cannot replace it.
